notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-21T01:25:25.503Z","harness:claude-code","Design as implemented, 2026-11-21. One sealed history_repair record carries bridge {version: 1, breaks: [{gap_from, gap_to, record_hash}]}. gap_from is the after_hash recorded before the break, gap_to the before_hash recorded after it, and record_hash belongs to the break entry (null for a legacy entry). Its patch is jsonPatch.compare(replay without the unrecorded change, on-disk item). Its before_hash is the last recorded after_hash, and its after_hash is the on-disk item hash, so replay re-anchors on the truth at the bridge.\n\nVerifier (verifyHistoryChainWithVersion, src/core/history/replay.ts): an anchored before-hash mismatch opens a segment only when the next bridge's first descriptor names exactly that break. Inside the segment each patch must still apply strictly, and each recorded link must hold or be the next named break. At the bridge, every named break must have occurred, its before_hash must link, and replay plus its patch must hash to its after_hash. Failures: bridge_invalid (malformed, unsealed or non-history_repair, pre-scanned so it wins over an earlier break), bridge_unnamed_break, bridge_names_absent_break, bridge_link_mismatch, bridge_after_hash_mismatch, bridge_without_break. The result adds bridged_segments. DRIFT_CACHE_VERSION 11 -> 12 so an older pm never trusts a verdict computed under these semantics.\n\nRepair (src/sdk/history/append-only-repair.ts): name the first replay mismatch plus every later recorded-link mismatch, build the record, and require the extended stream to verify before writing. The write appends to the exact bytes read under the lock and refuses non-UTF-8 streams. Refusal: history_append_only_repair_unavailable with reason chain_failure_not_a_break, patch_does_not_replay, item_missing, stream_not_utf8 or bridge_does_not_verify; next step is the rewriting repair. The pm health remedy for history_drift_chain_mismatch now names --append-only. --all passes through.\n\nRewrite interaction: reanchorHistoryEntries keeps a verified bridged segment byte-identical while nothing before it changed, so the rewriting repair is a no-op on a bridged stream. A rewrite before the segment, or any projected rewrite (merge union), dissolves the bridge into an ordinary record and keeps the original sealed record as rewrite evidence (retainPriorRecord), so a stale bridge never survives. Merge: two clones that bridged the same break (identical bridge, patch, anchors, version and algorithm) merge as equivalent_bridges. Ours stays byte-for-byte and theirs is appended as a neutral record with an empty patch and the original in evidence, so both repairs keep their provenance.\n\nKnown limit: versions between a break and its bridge lack the unrecorded change, so restore and get --at refuse them with history_replay_invalid. Versions before the break and from the bridge onward stay addressable. Cross-version: an older pm reports drift on a bridged stream, and its rewriting repair keeps the unknown bridge field. This pm then reports bridge_without_break, and its own rewriting repair dissolves the bridge."
